# i18n Translations Guide

## Two-layer system

| Layer | Hook | File | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Static UI | `useT()` | `src/i18n/ui.ts` | Buttons, labels, nav, static messages |
| Dynamic content | `useTranslate()` | `src/i18n/useTranslate.ts` | Lesson titles, exercise instructions |

**Never translate** exercise content (words, sentences, correct answers) — always stays Bulgarian.

### Core policy — hand-translate everything learner-facing (ALL levels)

> This policy is **global**: it applies identically to **A1, A2, B1, B2**. There is
> one translation standard for the whole platform — no per-level exceptions.

**Default = hand-translate.** Every learner-facing string is hand-translated on all
7 languages by a human. `useTranslate()` (Google) is now only a **temporary
fallback** shown while content is still being authored — it is **never** the
intended final state for shipped content. Treat any string still relying on
auto-translation as *unfinished*.

**Only exception — stays Bulgarian (never translated):** the exercise content
itself — the Bulgarian words, sentences, and correct answers the learner is
practising. (The dictionary *gloss* — the meaning of a Bulgarian word — IS
hand-translated; the Bulgarian headword stays Bulgarian.)

**What must be hand-translated (non-exhaustive):**

| Content | Where translations live |
|---|---|
| Exercise instructions | `instructionKey` → `ui.ts` (Philip) / `a2.ts` / `b1.ts` (level authors) |
| Exercise titles & subtitles | inline translation slot in the exercise object |
| Section titles & subtitles (`sectionStart`) | `titleI18n` / `subtitleI18n` (`Record<lang>`) |
| Grammar table titles, notes, column headers | inline translation slot / `Record<lang>` |
| Grammar reference | `Record<lang>` on all 7 languages (already required) |
| Culture / narrative sections | `Record<lang>` on all 7 languages |
| Lesson title & description, grammar topic tags | inline translation slot |
| Dictionary word glosses (meanings) | per-word translation slot |
| Test section names & exercise titles | same rules as lessons |

**Mechanism (plain version):** put the translations right next to the Bulgarian,
using whatever translation slot the field gives you — an `instructionKey` for
instructions, or a `Record<lang>` field (e.g. `titleI18n`) for inline content.
Always all 7 languages, always in order. **If a field has no translation slot
yet, Philip adds one to `src/content/shared/types.ts`** — never hack around a
missing slot or leave it on auto. When a slot is genuinely empty, the app falls
back to auto-translation so nothing breaks, but that content counts as not done.

**Rollout (no double work):** the *rule* is global and lives here; the *actual
translations* are per-level content. `instructionKey` strings are written by the
level's author in their own `src/i18n/<level>.ts`. Section titles
(`sectionStart.titleI18n`) arrive with the level-finalize pass, which is
Philip's — see [level-finalize.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/level-finalize.mdc).

**Status:** A1 ✅ and A2 ✅ (56 + 49 sections, all 6 languages, verified by the
`section-translations` lint rule). B1 has no sections yet — they come when the
level is content-complete. B2 not started.

## Third layer — the pre-translation cache

Everything without a translation slot (lesson descriptions, exercise titles,
dictionary glosses, test section names) is covered by a build-time pass that
writes `src/i18n/generated/translations.json`:

```
npm run pretranslate -- --level a2 --all
```

Runtime lookup order in `useTranslate()`: `translationOverrides.ts` →
`generated/translations.json` → `localStorage` cache → live Google Translate. An
empty slot therefore **never breaks the UI** — it just degrades in quality.

**The cache key is the raw Bulgarian string.** Two consequences worth knowing:

- Editing a Bulgarian sentence orphans its old entry and leaves the new one
  missing → that string falls back to live auto-translation.
- Re-running **without `--force`** only translates strings that aren't cached
  yet, so a delta pass after a round of corrections costs minutes, not hours.
  Run the full pass as soon as a level is content-complete; don't hold it back.

`VocabularyItem.translation` is intentionally left empty on every level — the
gloss comes from this cache at runtime. Don't fill it by hand.

## Checking translation completeness

Two deterministic lint rules cover what is impossible to eyeball across 7
languages:

```bash
npm run content:lint -- --rule section-translations     # sectionStart missing a language, bg in titleI18n, invalid theme
npm run content:lint -- --rule instruction-key-exists   # instructionKey undefined or missing a language
```

Both emit warnings, never errors, so they can't block a commit. Run them on any
change that touches section headers or instructions.

## Adding a new UI string

Edit [src/i18n/ui.ts](mdc:src/i18n/ui.ts). Add one line in the correct section:

```ts
'my.key': { bg: 'Текст', ar: 'نص', fr: 'Texte', en: 'Text', fa: 'متن', uk: 'Текст', ru: 'Текст' },
```

Language order: **bg, ar, fr, en, fa, uk, ru** — always this order, always all 7.

### Per-level translation files (A2 / B1 / B2 authors)

`ui.ts` is shared infrastructure — only Philip edits it directly. Level authors
add their `instructionKey` strings to a level-scoped file instead, which is
merged into `ui.ts` automatically:

| Level | File | Merged via |
|---|---|---|
| A2 (Nina) | `src/i18n/a2.ts` (`A2_TRANSLATIONS`) | `Object.assign(UI_TRANSLATIONS, A2_TRANSLATIONS)` in `ui.ts` |
| B1 (Alex) | `src/i18n/b1.ts` (`B1_TRANSLATIONS`) | `Object.assign(UI_TRANSLATIONS, B1_TRANSLATIONS)` in `ui.ts` |

Same shape and same 7-language rule as above. Prefix keys with the level
(`'a2.…'`, `'b1.…'`) to avoid collisions. Never duplicate an existing `ui.ts` key.

RTL languages: `ar` (Arabic) and `fa` (Farsi/Persian).

## Key naming conventions

```
nav.*          Navigation and sidebar items
auth.*         Login, register, profile
home.*         Home page strings
lesson.*       Lesson page UI (not lesson content)
exercise.*     Exercise interaction buttons and feedback
```

